WEBVTT

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:01.170
ANDREW LEADER: Welcome,

00:00:01.170 --> 00:00:03.660
Windows developers
and AI experts.

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:06.510
I wanted to start today's
session with a survey,

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:09.570
but since I can't see
you raise your hands,

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:12.990
click the like button if you've
ever opened a cloud invoice

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:15.780
and felt your stomach drop.

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:17.580
You might have experienced this

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:21.270
when adding cloud-backed
AI models to your app.

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:24.840
Your users end up loving the
feature and use it constantly,

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:28.590
which is awesome, but then
you get the bill for all

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:31.050
that cloud resource usage.

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:33.750
What if we could
change that story?

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:37.350
What if we could run the same
model locally using the hardware

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:41.430
the user already has and still
build the same amazing features

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:45.210
that users love without the
bill at the end of the day?

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:48.780
Using AI locally means
that instead of a bill,

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:54.180
you get better privacy, lower
latency, offline support,

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:57.420
and finally those cost
savings we mentioned.

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:01.770
Many top ISVs are already using
local AI on Windows to delight

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:04.200
and empower their users.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:07.950
Today, we're going to show you
how you can run your own custom

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:13.440
or open-source AI models locally
on Windows using Windows ML.

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:17.310
I'm Andrew Leader, a Product
Manager on the Windows ML team.

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:18.450
MAHA BAYANA: And
I'm Maha Bayana,

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:21.510
and I'm a Software Engineer
on the Windows ML team.

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:22.500
ANDREW LEADER: So Maha,

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:25.320
what models can you
use with Windows ML?

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:27.810
MAHA BAYANA: So Windows ML
works with ONNX models,

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:30.960
meaning you can choose from
over 40,000 ONNX models

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:33.600
from Hugging Face,
or you could choose

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:39.210
from 229,000 PyTorch models and
use the WinML CLI or PyTorch

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:41.880
to convert them to ONNX.

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:43.590
You can also train
your own models

00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:47.580
to achieve exactly what you need
and convert it to ONNX format.

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:51.750
So once you have an ONNX model,
you can run it on Windows ML,

00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:54.810
but if you want to run it on
the best possible across GPU

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:58.920
and NPU, you can even optimize
your model for each IHV.

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:02.340
So you can use the WinML CLI
to optimize your models,

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:06.780
and then the WinML allows your
app to accelerate your models

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:09.930
against a wide variety
of Windows hardware

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:13.530
without shipping specific SDKs
or each hardware variant.

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:14.760
