00:11:54.060 --> 00:11:57.060
So let's convert this web app
into a native WinUI 3 app

00:11:57.060 --> 00:11:59.190
in order to get that
floating overlay.

00:11:59.190 --> 00:12:01.170
ANDREW LEADER: Yeah, and we
can use the same ONNX model

00:12:01.170 --> 00:12:02.370
with that Windows app?

00:12:02.370 --> 00:12:02.790
MAHA BAYANA: Correct.

00:12:02.790 --> 00:12:04.530
So you can use the
same ONNX model

00:12:04.530 --> 00:12:07.950
that we downloaded
using the WinML CLI,

00:12:07.950 --> 00:12:12.570
pretty much just export it
the same way we did before.

00:12:12.570 --> 00:12:15.390
All right, so in this
WinUI 3 native project,

00:12:15.390 --> 00:12:20.430
I've installed the WinApp SDK ML
and WinApp SDK runtime packages,

00:12:20.430 --> 00:12:23.640
which adds WinML and
ONNX runtime to our app.

00:12:23.640 --> 00:12:27.570
So as you can see in the
packages part of this file,

00:12:27.570 --> 00:12:30.330
you can clearly see that we
have these packages installed

00:12:30.330 --> 00:12:31.200
over here.

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:32.970
ANDREW LEADER: Yeah, and
that runtime package,

00:12:32.970 --> 00:12:36.030
that adds the Windows App SDK
framework-dependent version,

00:12:36.030 --> 00:12:39.000
so our app can be smaller and
