00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:39.570
MAHA BAYANA: Correct.

00:12:39.570 --> 00:12:41.700
It saves about 40 MB of binaries

00:12:41.700 --> 00:12:44.010
that we'd otherwise have
to carry in our app.

00:12:44.010 --> 00:12:45.030
ANDREW LEADER: Nice.

00:12:45.030 --> 00:12:48.300
Okay, so once we have those
installed, what's next?

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:50.430
MAHA BAYANA: So next we
have our app install

00:12:50.430 --> 00:12:53.460
and register the WinML
execution providers.

00:12:53.460 --> 00:12:56.340
ANDREW LEADER: Okay, I've heard
about execution providers.

00:12:56.340 --> 00:13:01.650
I think those are SDKs built by
the hardware vendors like AMD,

00:13:01.650 --> 00:13:04.890
Intel, NVIDIA, and
Qualcomm, and they allow us

00:13:04.890 --> 00:13:07.500
to accelerate our
models on their NPUs,

00:13:07.500 --> 00:13:09.540
GPUs, and CPUs, right?

00:13:09.540 --> 00:13:09.960
MAHA BAYANA: Correct.

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:12.360
So WinML allows us

00:13:12.360 --> 00:13:14.490
to dynamically acquire
these providers,

00:13:14.490 --> 00:13:17.070
meaning that our app
doesn't need to carry 80 MB

00:13:17.070 --> 00:13:19.890
of providers, further
reducing our app size

00:13:19.890 --> 00:13:22.320
and simplifying
our distribution.

00:13:22.320 --> 00:13:23.610
ANDREW LEADER: Awesome.

00:13:23.610 --> 00:13:24.930
MAHA BAYANA: So we
can use a few steps

00:13:24.930 --> 00:13:27.270
in our code to
acquire these EPs.

00:13:27.270 --> 00:13:30.900
So let's go ahead and scroll
down to this part over here.

00:13:30.900 --> 00:13:34.440
So first, we're going to create
our ORT environment using this

00:13:34.440 --> 00:13:36.600
particular line over here,

00:13:36.600 --> 00:13:41.460
which essentially just creates
an ORT environment instance.

00:13:41.460 --> 00:13:43.380
So once we've done that,
for step number two,

00:13:43.380 --> 00:13:46.590
we're going to have to
discover the compatible EPs

00:13:46.590 --> 00:13:48.870
with our execution-provided
catalog.

00:13:48.870 --> 00:13:51.540
So we're going to jump down
to this particular method

00:13:51.540 --> 00:13:54.510
over here, and you can
see that we're going

00:13:54.510 --> 00:13:58.020
to be using the Find All
Providers API in the list

00:13:58.020 --> 00:14:02.940
to essentially list all of
the execution providers

00:14:02.940 --> 00:14:04.860
that are available to us.

00:14:04.860 --> 00:14:08.520
Now, for today's session, we're
going to be using the QNN EP.

00:14:08.520 --> 00:14:10.350
So we're going to
see through all

00:14:10.350 --> 00:14:14.670
of these execution provider
lists that we have in a for loop

00:14:14.670 --> 00:14:18.210
and look for our specific
EP, which is a QNN one.

00:14:18.210 --> 00:14:21.780
So once we have our QNN
EP, for step number three,

00:14:21.780 --> 00:14:25.140
we're going to go ahead and
call EnsureReadyAsync API

00:14:25.140 --> 00:14:27.060
on our particular EP.

00:14:27.060 --> 00:14:31.140
Now, this is essentially going
to help make that EP ready.

00:14:31.140 --> 00:14:34.830
And once that EP is ready, we're
going to go to step number four,

00:14:34.830 --> 00:14:38.040
which is essentially registering
that EP to our environment.

00:14:38.040 --> 00:14:41.070
So we're going to go ahead
and use this API down here,

00:14:41.070 --> 00:14:46.800
which is TryRegister in order to
register our EP to ONNX Runtime.

00:14:46.800 --> 00:14:47.280
ANDREW LEADER: Got it.

00:14:47.280 --> 00:14:49.560
And so, if the EPs
aren't on the device,

00:14:49.560 --> 00:14:52.590
those actually get pulled
down through Windows Update.

00:14:52.590 --> 00:14:53.490
Is that correct?

00:14:53.490 --> 00:14:54.060
MAHA BAYANA: Correct.

00:14:54.060 --> 00:14:54.330
ANDREW LEADER: All right.

00:14:54.330 --> 00:14:57.840
Now that we have those EPs on
the device, how do we inference

00:14:57.840 --> 00:14:59.610
against a specific EP?

00:14:59.610 --> 00:15:00.150
MAHA BAYANA: Yeah.

00:15:00.150 --> 00:15:03.570
So to run a model inference
using a specific EP,

00:15:03.570 --> 00:15:06.600
we need to use something
called as session options.

00:15:06.600 --> 00:15:10.500
Now, we can set quite a few
metrics in the session options.

00:15:10.500 --> 00:15:12.600
So let's go ahead and
scroll down to this part,

00:15:12.600 --> 00:15:15.900
which essentially
sets those metrics.

00:15:15.900 --> 00:15:18.480
So over here, we can see
that for step number one,

00:15:18.480 --> 00:15:22.410
we're kind of using that API
called GetEPDevices in order

00:15:22.410 --> 00:15:26.520
for it to give us all our
EP and device combinations.

00:15:26.520 --> 00:15:27.930
So the one that we're
going to be using

00:15:27.930 --> 00:15:29.220
for today's session is going

00:15:29.220 --> 00:15:32.610
to be our QNN EP
with the NPU device.

00:15:32.610 --> 00:15:34.920
So we're going to see through
all of those combinations

00:15:34.920 --> 00:15:38.310
over here in order to look
for that specific one.

00:15:38.310 --> 00:15:40.560
So for step number three,
we're going to go ahead

00:15:40.560 --> 00:15:44.310
and use our Append
Execution Providers API.

00:15:44.310 --> 00:15:47.340
And we're essentially
going to get that QNN NPU

00:15:47.340 --> 00:15:48.870
that we found from earlier.

00:15:48.870 --> 00:15:51.960
And we're going to append it to
this particular session options.

00:15:51.960 --> 00:15:53.520
So we can tell our
inference session,

00:15:53.520 --> 00:15:56.320
this is what we
want you to run on.

00:15:57.630 --> 00:16:01.170
And then, we can also set
a couple of other options

00:16:01.170 --> 00:16:02.670
with our session options.

00:16:02.670 --> 00:16:06.090
For example, if I don't want
my session to run on the CPU

00:16:06.090 --> 00:16:07.980
and I completely
want to disable it,

00:16:07.980 --> 00:16:12.270
I can use this
AddSessionConfigEntry API.

00:16:12.270 --> 00:16:15.240
And in this API, as
you can see over here,

00:16:15.240 --> 00:16:19.170
we're essentially just saying
disable the CPU fallback.

00:16:19.170 --> 00:16:22.380
And, yeah, you can also
use some other APIs

00:16:22.380 --> 00:16:27.000
like SetExecutionPolicy
or SetEPPolicy.

00:16:27.000 --> 00:16:29.490
And that will essentially
help you prefer one sort

00:16:29.490 --> 00:16:31.050
of hardware over the other.

00:16:31.050 --> 00:16:34.440
So in this case, that would
be our NPU over the CPU.

00:16:34.440 --> 00:16:35.220
ANDREW LEADER: Excellent.

00:16:35.220 --> 00:16:37.680
Yeah, that code looks pretty
similar to our web code.

00:16:37.680 --> 00:16:40.470
It's pretty neat how we can use
