00:17:47.340 --> 00:17:51.600
Speaking of what's new
with Windows ML in 2026,

00:17:51.600 --> 00:17:54.270
we started today with
the Windows ML CLI tool,

00:17:54.270 --> 00:17:57.810
which is a new tool that helps
you convert and optimize models

00:17:57.810 --> 00:18:03.750
to run locally on Windows
across CPUs, GPUs, and NPUs.

00:18:03.750 --> 00:18:07.500
The Windows ML ORT Gen AI
library has also received a

00:18:07.500 --> 00:18:09.570
number of performance updates,

00:18:09.570 --> 00:18:13.980
leading to faster local LLM
inferencing on Windows.

00:18:13.980 --> 00:18:18.030
And Windows ML itself has
been updated to version 2.0,

00:18:18.030 --> 00:18:21.930
bringing the ONNX Runtime
1.24, which includes a number

00:18:21.930 --> 00:18:24.300
of performance and
stability updates,

00:18:24.300 --> 00:18:27.960
and some newly updated
plugin-based execution providers

00:18:27.960 --> 00:18:29.910
that have performance
updates and allow you

00:18:29.910 --> 00:18:31.830
to use the execution providers

00:18:31.830 --> 00:18:36.240
across different runtime
versions more easily.

00:18:36.240 --> 00:18:41.130
And finally, WebNN is being
updated to use Windows ML

00:18:41.130 --> 00:18:43.650
when on Windows,
as we saw earlier,

00:18:43.650 --> 00:18:46.230
leading to faster
hardware-accelerated machine

00:18:46.230 --> 00:18:51.300
learning model inferencing when
on Edge or Chrome browsers.

00:18:51.300 --> 00:18:54.060
And if you're looking to try
Windows ML for yourself,

00:18:54.060 --> 00:18:56.610
you can install the
AI Dev Gallery app.

00:18:56.610 --> 00:18:58.440
It includes numerous samples

00:18:58.440 --> 00:19:02.460
of various AI models running
locally via Windows ML,

00:19:02.460 --> 00:19:03.990
and it includes code samples,

00:19:03.990 --> 00:19:08.040
so you can easily use those
models in your own apps.

00:19:08.040 --> 00:19:09.690
And finally, I wanted

00:19:09.690 --> 00:19:13.710
to highlight some other local
AI solutions on Windows.

00:19:13.710 --> 00:19:16.770
Windows ML is part of the
Microsoft Foundry on Windows,

00:19:16.770 --> 00:19:20.820
which provides three
main local AI solutions.

00:19:20.820 --> 00:19:24.060
If you're not a machine
learning expert like Maha,

00:19:24.060 --> 00:19:27.810
you should check out the Windows
AI APIs in Foundry Local,

00:19:27.810 --> 00:19:30.360
both of which provide
ready-to-use AI models

00:19:30.360 --> 00:19:33.930
that require no machine
learning expertise.

00:19:33.930 --> 00:19:37.560
These models are pre-optimized
and installed system-wide,

00:19:37.560 --> 00:19:40.710
allowing you to focus on
writing your code rather

00:19:40.710 --> 00:19:42.930
than optimizing models.

00:19:42.930 --> 00:19:47.130
But if you need something custom
that you can't find in AI APIs

00:19:47.130 --> 00:19:49.680
or Foundry Local, you
can use Windows ML

00:19:49.680 --> 00:19:54.330
to run any custom or
open-source model.

